WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.320
Operators need enough visibility to distinguish service problems, model problems, client problems, and hardware problems.

2
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:19.362
When a local AI workflow fails, the visible symptom may be vague: a slow response, a missing model, an application timeout, or a user report that the tool feels different.

3
00:00:19.362 --> 00:00:24.961
Troubleshooting is much easier when the environment has a simple operating picture.

4
00:00:24.961 --> 00:00:33.359
Useful troubleshooting starts with service status, logs, model inventory, storage availability, and resource utilization.

5
00:00:33.359 --> 00:00:36.936
Is the service running? Is the expected model present?

6
00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:40.435
Is there enough disk space? Is memory under pressure?

7
00:00:40.435 --> 00:00:43.157
Is CPU or GPU utilization unusually high?

8
00:00:43.157 --> 00:00:45.412
Did a configuration value change?

9
00:00:45.412 --> 00:00:52.488
These basic checks can explain many issues before teams start changing application code or model settings.

10
00:00:52.488 --> 00:01:03.763
Slow responses may come from model loading, insufficient memory, long prompts, high concurrency, CPU or GPU pressure, thermal throttling, or client-side expectations.

11
00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:12.239
Failed pulls or missing models may point to connectivity, permissions, naming, storage, or environment configuration issues.

12
00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:19.081
A troubleshooting checklist can reduce repeated support work and help new operators build confidence.

13
00:01:19.081 --> 00:01:23.591
Logging should support investigation without creating a data leak.

14
00:01:23.591 --> 00:01:30.745
Logs may reveal service errors, model loading behavior, configuration problems, or integration failures.

15
00:01:30.745 --> 00:01:38.366
They should not casually store secrets, credentials, personal data, confidential prompts, or sensitive outputs.

16
00:01:38.366 --> 00:01:46.453
The best troubleshooting practice is to gather enough evidence to act while still respecting privacy and data boundaries.
